    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is true that managed conservation offers practical tools for protecting specific, vulnerable species, yet its reliance on human-centric wildlife corridors and veterinary intervention often inadvertently restricts the very genetic diversity required for long-term resilience. Critics of rewilding sometimes overlook that maintaining species in isolation can reduce adaptability, whereas allowing wider ecological processes to function supports the natural recovery of plant life and soil health. By limiting ecological exchange between protected areas, current practices may inadvertently create isolated pockets that are ill-equipped to withstand shifting climate patterns or emerging threats. Emphasizing these nuances demonstrates that while traditional methods have merits, they lack the comprehensive, adaptive capacity of a fully wild ecosystem.",
      "source": "target",
